WEBVTT

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:21.590
<v Ira Glass>OK, here's a question that I wasn't sure if it was appropriate to ask a priest. But I had a priest in the studio, and I asked the question. And the priest, Father Tom Santa from St. Michael's Church in Old Town in Chicago, was kind enough to humor me. My question was, in confession, do people ever confess things to you that it's hard not to roll your eyes at?
<v Ira Glass>好的，这是一个我不太确定是否适合问神父的问题。但我当时工作室里正好有一位神父，我就问了。这位神父是来自芝加哥老城圣迈克尔教堂的汤姆·圣塔神父，他很友好地满足了我的好奇心。我的问题是，在告解时，有没有人向你告解一些让你很难不翻白眼的事情？

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:44.980
<v Fr. Tom Santa>[LAUGHS] In front of them, no. But yes. I had one person-- and I won't tell you what city, because he might be listening-- who would come in and every single confession would confess that he laughed in church. And I asked him, why do you laugh in church? He said, because Father tells jokes.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>[笑] 在他们面前，不会。但确实有。我遇到过一个人——我不会告诉你他在哪个城市，因为他可能正在听——他每次来告解，告解的内容都是他在教堂里笑了。我问他，你为什么要在教堂里笑？他说，因为神父讲了笑话。

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.200
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑]

00:00:46.200 --> 00:01:02.700
<v Fr. Tom Santa>And I said, Father wants you to laugh in church. Well, you're not allowed to laugh in church. If you're not allowed to laugh in church, then Father wouldn't be telling you jokes. I know. That's his problem, not mine. [LAUGHS]
<v Fr. Tom Santa>我说，神父是希望你在教堂里笑的。嗯，教堂里是不准笑的。如果教堂里不准笑，那神父就不会给你讲笑话了。我知道。那是他的问题，不是我的。[笑]

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:15.240
<v Ira Glass>Well, today we have a whole program about confessions. We have a story about somebody who does confess and a story about somebody who refuses to confess. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , by the way, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>那么，今天我们的整个节目都关于“告解”。我们有一个关于某人确实告解的故事，还有一个关于某人拒绝告解的故事。这是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》节目，顺便说一下，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:29.540
<v Ira Glass>And we called Father Santa to start the show because Father Santa is an expert in something called scrupulosity, which takes a number of different kinds of forms. But among Catholics, it results in people confessing to things they do not need to confess.
<v Ira Glass>我们请圣塔神父来开启这个节目，因为圣塔神父是一位研究“拘泥症”的专家，这种病症有多种表现形式。但在天主教徒中，它表现为人们会去告解一些根本不需要告解的事情。

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:44.350
<v Ira Glass>Scrupulosity is when somebody obsesses over whether he is doing things that are sinful. It causes anxiety. The person can't stop thinking about it. It's something that the Catholic church has been noting and dealing with for hundreds of years. A number of saints suffered from this.
<v Ira Glass>拘泥症是指一个人过度沉迷于自己是否在做罪恶之事。这会导致焦虑。当事人无法停止思考这件事。这是天主教会几百年来一直注意并处理的问题。许多圣徒也曾饱受其苦。

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:53.420
<v Ira Glass>Father Santa says that it can be paralyzing for people. It can make the smallest decisions impossible. He remembers one woman that he spoke to when he was just starting out as a priest.
<v Ira Glass>圣塔神父说，这会让人们感到瘫痪。它会让最小的决定都变得不可能。他记得他刚做神父时，曾与一位女士交谈。

00:01:53.420 --> 00:02:06.290
<v Fr. Tom Santa>She called the rectory. I answered the phone, and she identified who she was. And she says, Father, I'm trying to decide if I should make tomato soup or chicken noodle soup for my husband for dinner. Which one is not a sin?
<v Fr. Tom Santa>她给牧师住宅打了电话。我接了电话，她自报了家门。她说，神父，我正在试图决定今晚该给丈夫做番茄汤还是鸡汤面。哪一种才不算犯罪？

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:15.620
<v Fr. Tom Santa>It's because she felt that if she prepared the wrong soup, she would disappoint her husband or her children or her guest. And that's what's displeasing to God.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>这是因为她觉得如果她做了错误的汤，她就会让丈夫、孩子或客人失望。而那正是让上帝不悦的事。

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:20.760
<v Ira Glass>Because God would be displeased if her family were upset.
<v Ira Glass>因为如果她的家人感到不开心，上帝会不高兴。

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.680
<v Fr. Tom Santa>That's right. Right. Yeah. There must, therefore, be a sin.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>没错。对。是的。因此，那一定是一种罪。

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.450
<v Ira Glass>How did you respond?
<v Ira Glass>你是怎么回应的？

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:34.250
<v Fr. Tom Santa>I responded by I think I told her to pick the tomato soup. [LAUGHS]. I just made the decision.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>我回应时……我想我告诉她选番茄汤。[笑]。我就直接做了决定。

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:44.340
<v Fr. Tom Santa>And I was young. I was newly ordained. I didn't realize anything. I didn't realize anything more than that she seemingly was asking me a question.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>那时我很年轻。我刚被任命。我什么都不懂。我除了意识到她似乎在问我一个问题之外，什么都没意识到。

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:59.990
<v Fr. Tom Santa>It wasn't until later, when I was talking to other priests, that I realized that I had encountered my first scrupulous person and that my answer was, in fact, not very pastoral, but pretty flippant. So I've tried to outgrow being flippant.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>直到后来，当我与其他神父交谈时，我才意识到我遇到了我的第一个拘泥症患者，而我的回答，事实上，一点也不牧灵，反而相当轻率。所以我努力改掉这种轻率。

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:07.680
<v Ira Glass>It's funny. You're saying it's a bad answer. But at another level, it's a perfectly helpful and good answer, because it took her out of her pain.
<v Ira Glass>很有趣。你说这是一个糟糕的回答。但在另一个层面上，这是一个非常有用且好的回答，因为它让她从痛苦中解脱出来了。

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.120
<v Fr. Tom Santa>Until the next time she opened the cabinet, yes.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>直到她下次打开橱柜的时候，是的。

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.860
<v Ira Glass>Oh, good. You see, that's why you're a priest.
<v Ira Glass>哦，好的。你看，这就是为什么你是神父。

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:19.620
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHTER]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:32.360
<v Ira Glass>Father  Santa has written a book for sufferers. He leads retreats. He edits a newsletter, which people send questions to. Like for instance, you're supposed to fast for an hour before taking Communion, which Father Santa says is pretty easy.
<v Ira Glass>圣塔神父为受苦者写了一本书。他主持静修会。他编辑一份时事通讯，人们会给它寄去问题。例如，你应该在领圣体前禁食一小时，圣塔神父说这其实很简单。

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:54.800
<v Fr. Tom Santa>Anybody who goes to church, it's about an hour before you receive communion. But anyways, if they  have scrupulosity, they'll be concerned that for instance, as they're sitting there in church, they discovered that they had a seed left in their mouth and they swallowed it. Does that break their fast? Or does lipstick break your fast? Or things like that.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>任何去教堂的人，这大约是你领圣体前的一小时。但不管怎样，如果他们有拘泥症，他们会担心，比如，当他们坐在教堂里时，他们发现嘴里还留着一颗种子，然后把它吞下去了。这会打破他们的禁食吗？或者口红会打破你的禁食吗？或者诸如此类的事情。

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:55.360
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:06.130
<v Fr. Tom Santa>Another one I get commonly is, we're going to go on a cruise and there's no guarantee that we'll have Sunday Mass. So am I committing a mortal sin by skipping Sunday Mass?
<v Fr. Tom Santa>另一个我经常收到的问题是，我们要去游轮旅行，不能保证会有主日弥撒。那么我不参加主日弥撒是在犯大罪吗？

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:14.070
<v Fr. Tom Santa>I said no, you're on a boat, and that's what you do. Enjoy your trip. Have a great time. Don't worry about it.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>我说没有，你在船上，这就是你该做的。享受你的旅程吧。玩得开心。不用担心这个。

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:19.190
<v Ira Glass>Somebody wrote your newsletter, I sometimes get frustrated with my dog. Is this a serious sin?
<v Ira Glass>有人写信给你的时事通讯问，我有时会对我的狗感到沮丧。这是严重的罪吗？

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:29.970
<v Fr. Tom Santa>Right. See, and what that is is what they're really saying, it's not the dog. What they're really saying is, I felt an emotion. And is that emotion acceptable or unacceptable?
<v Fr. Tom Santa>对。你看，他们真正想说的不是狗的事。他们真正想说的是，我产生了一种情绪。这种情绪是可以接受的还是不可接受的？

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:36.920
<v Ira Glass>Somebody wrote your newsletter, is it a sin to exaggerate a little when telling a story to friends? I only mean to add a little color.
<v Ira Glass>有人写信给你的时事通讯问，给朋友讲故事时稍微夸大一点是一种罪吗？我只是想增加一点色彩。

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.160
<v Fr. Tom Santa>Right. It's called a skill.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>对。这叫技巧。

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:39.670
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑]

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:44.750
<v Fr. Tom Santa>There's a lot of people who make lots of money with that ability.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>有很多人靠这种能力赚了很多钱。

00:04:44.750 --> 00:05:05.110
<v Ira Glass>Father Santa says that there are two different kinds of scrupulosity. There are the people who are just not sure what is a sin and what is not a sin, and they're getting very stressed out about that. That is pretty straightforward to deal with, he says. A priest just needs to patiently talk these people through what counts as a sin. And usually over the course of months, the anxieties fade.
<v Ira Glass>圣塔神父说，拘泥症有两种不同的类型。有些人只是不确定什么是罪，什么不是罪，他们为此感到非常紧张。他说，处理这种情况相当直接。神父只需要耐心地向这些人解释什么是罪。通常在几个月的时间里，焦虑就会消退。

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:26.810
<v Ira Glass>Much more difficult are the people who have an actual psychological disorder, whose scrupulosity is tied up with what priests have come to understand in the last three or four decades is obsessive compulsive disorder. Like for instance, a man who felt that he could not get out of bed in the morning until he believed that he was doing it in a pure way that would not offend God. Or people who believe that they are constantly blaspheming God.
<v Ira Glass>更难处理的是那些患有实际心理障碍的人，他们的拘泥症与神父在过去三四十年里开始理解的强迫症联系在一起。例如，一个男人觉得他在确信自己是以一种不会冒犯上帝的纯洁方式起床之前，都无法起床。或者那些认为自己不断在亵渎上帝的人。

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:36.300
<v Ira Glass>Or a woman who thought that if she left her house unlocked, it would put her whole family in danger, which she believed was a sin. So she would check the lock over and over and over and over.
<v Ira Glass>或者一个女人认为如果她不锁好门，就会让她的全家处于危险之中，她认为这是一种罪。所以她会一遍又一遍地检查门锁。

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:53.920
<v Ira Glass>Father Santa says that in confession, these people describe their sins without feeling. They do it almost mechanically. They ask the same questions over and over, not accepting the answers that they're given. He calls this the scrupulous ritual. He says that the only thing that can help these people is therapy, behavior modification, drugs.
<v Ira Glass>圣塔神父说，在告解时，这些人描述他们的罪时没有感情。他们几乎机械地这样做。他们反复问同样的问题，不接受给他们的回答。他称之为“拘泥仪式”。他说，唯一能帮助这些人的是治疗、行为矫正、药物。

00:05:53.920 --> 00:06:11.040
<v Ira Glass>And Father Santa says that unfortunately, these are not people who usually go for that. On a retreat with 20 people, he'll maybe be able to convince one or two of them to seek that kind of professional help. The ones who refuse sometimes tell him that he's just trying to let them off too easy for their sins.
<v Ira Glass>圣塔神父说，不幸的是，这些人通常不去寻求这些帮助。在有20人的静修会上，他或许能说服其中一两个寻求那种专业帮助。那些拒绝的人有时告诉他，他只是想让他们因为自己的罪而逃避惩罚。

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:22.440
<v Fr. Tom Santa>They are convinced that it is God's will for them that they suffer this way. Accept it as their personal cross. And somehow they have to live like this.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>他们确信这是上帝的旨意，让他们这样受苦。接受它作为他们的个人十字架。他们不知何故必须这样生活。

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:30.120
<v Ira Glass>Now, you have an example in your book about you got a letter in a box, and it was 150 pages. Is that what it was?
<v Ira Glass>现在，你的书里有一个例子，你收到了一个盒子里装的信，有150页长。是这样的吗？

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:31.960
<v Fr. Tom Santa>Yes it was. It was the most incredible--
<v Fr. Tom Santa>是的，确实如此。这是最不可思议的——

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:35.440
<v Ira Glass>And just explain what this was. Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>解释一下这是什么。是的。

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:46.300
<v Fr. Tom Santa>At the time, I was publisher of our publishing house, Liguori Publications in St. Louis. And I thought it was a manuscript that had appeared on my desk for publication, so I started reading it.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>当时，我是圣路易斯利古里出版社的出版人。我以为那是一份出现在我办公桌上供出版的手稿，所以我开始阅读它。

00:06:46.300 --> 00:07:08.530
<v Fr. Tom Santa>And it was the most unbelievable 150 pages describing this person's struggle with sin, with sexuality, everything that you could possibly imagine. 150 pages in the most unbelievable amount of details, all summarized by chapters with questions that would go with it.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>那是最令人难以置信的150页，描述了这个人与罪、与性、所有你能想象到的一切的斗争。150页的内容，有着令人难以置信的细节，所有内容都按章节总结，并附有与之相关的问题。

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:20.200
<v Fr. Tom Santa>And the closing paragraph said, I have two questions for you. One, would you be willing to hear my confession? And the second one is, do you think I'm scrupulous?
<v Fr. Tom Santa>结束语写道：我有两个问题想问你。第一，你愿意听我的告解吗？第二个是，你认为我有拘泥症吗？

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:23.640
<v Ira Glass>Was it surprising to you that this person was even asking the question?
<v Ira Glass>这个人在问这个问题，让你感到惊讶吗？

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:46.890
<v Fr. Tom Santa>No. It wasn't surprising to me. What was surprising to me was the level of commitment, first of all, that was required in order to write that and the level of suffering that it expressed. Every page was filled with suffering. And that's what hit me more than anything else. And it certainly isn't God's will for us. There's no way that's God's will.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>不。我并不感到惊讶。让我惊讶的是，首先，写下这些所需要的承诺水平，以及它所表达的痛苦程度。每一页都充满了痛苦。这就是比任何其他事情都更打动我的地方。这绝不是上帝对我们的旨意。这绝不可能是上帝的旨意。

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:59.910
<v Ira Glass>Confession, Father Santa says, can be such  a satisfying part of a priest's job. To help somebody heal and cope and get closer to God, it's at the heart of why some people become priests in the first place. It can be very intense.
<v Ira Glass>圣塔神父说，告解可以是神父工作中非常令人满足的一部分。帮助某人治愈、应对并更接近上帝，这是某些人成为神父的核心原因。这可能会非常强烈。

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:12.410
<v Fr. Tom Santa>And I can't remember many weddings and baptisms clearly. But there are confessions over the years that I can remember very, very clearly as if they happened yesterday.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>我记不清很多婚礼和洗礼了。但多年来，有些告解我记得非常非常清楚，就像昨天发生的一样。

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:13.030
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:31.430
<v Fr. Tom Santa>It's also frustrating when you can't do it. When you just can't do it. There's no word that you can say that will bring healing. There's nothing that you can say that will bring people to a sense of peace. That's frustrating, because you want it so bad for them.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>当你做不到的时候，也很沮丧。当你就是做不到的时候。没有任何话语可以说能带来治愈。没有任何话语可以说能给人们带来内心的平静。那很令人沮丧，因为你太想为他们做到了。

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:34.160
<v Ira Glass>And that's what can be so hard with a scrupulous confession.
<v Ira Glass>这就是拘泥症告解最困难的地方。

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:41.800
<v Fr. Tom Santa>That's the hard part about it. Especially if you're sitting in the confessional and you've already been in the confessional for an hour, an hour and a half.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>那是最难的部分。特别是如果你坐在告解室里，而你已经在告解室里待了一个小时、一个半小时了。

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:55.550
<v Fr. Tom Santa>And you're extremely vulnerable when you're in the confessional. You never know what the person is that's going to come in, what they're going to talk about. You never know if it's something that's relatively unimportant or it's the deepest, darkest secret. You never know.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>当你坐在告解室里时，你非常脆弱。你永远不知道进来的会是什么样的人，他们会谈论什么。你永远不知道这只是一件相对不重要的事情，还是最深沉、最黑暗的秘密。你永远不知道。

00:08:55.550 --> 00:09:24.550
<v Fr. Tom Santa>And it's very draining to give individual energy to each person that comes in, to listen to them with a patient, loving heart. And so after an hour and a half, for instance, and you end up with a scrupulous person that comes in, and all they're doing is engaging in this scrupulous ritual, that has got to be one of the most difficult things I've ever done as a priest, to sit there and be patient and loving. Because all I really want to do is just slam the door and forget it.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>给进来的每个人提供个人的精力，用耐心、充满爱的心去倾听他们，这非常消耗体力。所以，例如一个半小时后，你最终遇到了一个拘泥症患者，而他们所做的只是沉浸在这种拘泥仪式中，这一定是我做神父以来做过的最困难的事情之一，坐在那里保持耐心和爱心。因为我真正想做的只是关上门，把它忘掉。

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:33.630
<v Fr. Tom Santa>But you can't. You can't. You have to wait and be patient and give them the love that they really, really desire and need.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>但你不能。你不能。你必须等待，保持耐心，给予他们真正、真正渴望和需要的爱。

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:36.400
<v Ira Glass>These people really test you.
<v Ira Glass>这些人确实在考验你。

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:51.980
<v Fr. Tom Santa>Yes. Our own saint Alphonsus Liguori, who was scrupulous, said he exhausted the confessors in Naples, the city he was from. Because whenever they saw Alphonsus enter into the city, they would all run because he would exhaust them.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>是的。我们自己的圣阿尔方索·利古里，他自己就有拘泥症，他说他让那不勒斯的告解神父们筋疲力尽，那是他所在的城市。因为每当他们看到阿尔方索进城时，他们都会逃跑，因为他会让他们筋疲力尽。

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:55.070
<v Fr. Tom Santa>[LAUGHTER]
<v Fr. Tom Santa>[笑声]

00:09:55.070 --> 00:10:04.190
<v Fr. Tom Santa>And he even admitted that he would do that.
<v Fr. Tom Santa>他甚至承认他会那样做。

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:19.660
<v Ira Glass>Father Tom Santa. His book is Understanding Scrupulosity, Questions, Helps, and Encouragement. His newsletter you can find online is "Scrupulous Anonymous."
<v Ira Glass>汤姆·圣塔神父。他的书是《理解拘泥症：问题、帮助与鼓励》。你可以在网上找到他的时事通讯“拘泥匿名者”。

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:36.280
<v Ira Glass>Act One, "Kim Possible." So this is the story of a criminal investigation that hinges entirely on a confession. And I am not going to pretend that this goes well. The case falls apart for some really interesting reasons. Here's Saul Elbein with the story.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“金·波西布尔”。这是一个完全取决于告解的刑事调查故事。我不会假装事情进展顺利。这个案件因为一些非常有趣的原因分崩离析了。这是索尔·埃尔本带来的故事。

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:43.980
