<v Jim Trainum>我们现在在处理什么案子？谋杀，对吧？

00:20:50.510 --> 00:20:52.050
<v Kim>No, a forgery.
<v Kim>不，伪造。

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:53.000
<v Jim Trainum>OK.
<v Jim Trainum>好的。

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:55.380
<v Kim>The lieutenant told me forgery.
<v Kim>中尉告诉我伪造。

00:20:55.380 --> 00:21:00.590
<v Saul Elbein>All those details Jim had been fixated on all those years? He was the one  who gave them to her.
<v Saul Elbein>吉姆多年来一直盯着看的所有那些细节？他就是那个把它们喂给她的人。

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:03.180
<v Jim Trainum>Do you remember any of the items that you bought at People's?
<v Jim Trainum>你记得你在人民药店买了什么吗？

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:06.280
<v Kim>No.
<v Kim>不。

00:21:06.280 --> 00:21:19.550
<v Saul Elbein>Like most of us, Jim had an image of the sort of cops who force false confessions. Bad apples. Cops who knowingly railroad some innocent to get the DA off their backs, or because they just don't care enough to do their jobs right.
<v Saul Elbein>像我们大多数人一样，吉姆对那种强迫虚假告解的警察有一种刻板印象。害群之马。那些故意冤枉无辜者以摆脱地区检察官的压力，或者因为他们根本不在乎是否做好工作的警察。

00:21:19.550 --> 00:21:31.810
<v Saul Elbein>But now suddenly Jim realized he was a bad apple. And he wondered, how many other cases had he messed up? How many more confessions had he unknowingly coerced? Kim's interrogation was the only one he'd ever recorded.
<v Saul Elbein>但现在吉姆突然意识到他自己就是害群之马。他纳闷，他搞砸了多少其他案件？还有多少告解是他无意中强迫出来的？金的审讯是他唯一录下来的。

00:21:31.810 --> 00:21:51.660
<v Jim Trainum>I just felt bad. I mean, I thought I was a very conscientious, very methodical, very detail-oriented detective. And I would never, ever want to feed information or contaminate a suspect. And here I am doing it plain as day.
<v Jim Trainum>我只是感觉很糟糕。我是说，我以为我是一个非常有良心、非常有条理、非常注重细节的侦探。我绝不想喂养信息或污染嫌疑人。而我却在这里明目张胆地这样做。

00:21:51.660 --> 00:22:15.570
<v Saul Elbein>Over the years, Jim has watched these tapes dozens of times and has catalogued all the different ways that he and the other interrogators inadvertently led her into a false confession. At some point, Kim had heard, as she was facing 30 years in prison, how she might never get her kids back, and decided to give them what they wanted. So she stitched together the little pieces Jim and the other interrogators slipped her to make up a story they'd believe.
<v Saul Elbein>多年来，吉姆看了这些录像带几十次，并分类了所有他和审讯人员无意中引导她做出虚假告解的不同方式。在某个时刻，金在面临30年监禁的情况下，听说了她可能再也见不到孩子了，决定给他们想要的东西。所以她把吉姆和其他审讯人员递给她的零散碎片拼接起来，编成了一个他们会相信的故事。

00:22:15.570 --> 00:22:33.510
<v Saul Elbein>Now when I say slipped, we're not talking about a little bit of information. We're talking about a lot. Kim's interrogation started around 8:00 AM and went until 1:00 AM the next day. And as the day wore on, the interrogators started to get tired and sloppy. Sometimes they just came out and told her actual details.
<v Saul Elbein>现在当我说“递给”时，我们谈论的不是一点点信息。我们谈论的是很多。金的审讯从早上8点左右一直持续到第二天凌晨1点。随着时间的推移，审讯人员开始感到疲倦和草率。有时他们只是直接出来告诉她实际的细节。

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:47.720
<v Saul Elbein>At one point, one of the interrogators got so frustrated trying to convince Kim that they knew what she did, that he told her. He said to her, we know what you ate. Kim responded, huh? What did I eat? The interrogator said, Chinese food.
<v Saul Elbein>有一次，其中一名审讯人员在试图说服金他们知道她做了什么时感到非常沮丧，他告诉了她。他对她说，我们知道你吃了什么。金回答说，嗯？我吃了什么？审讯人员说，中餐。

00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:49.850
<v Detective>We know what you ate.
<v Detective>我们知道你吃了什么。

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:50.650
<v Kim>Huh?
<v Kim>嗯？

00:22:50.650 --> 00:22:51.960
<v Detective>We know what you ate!
<v Detective>我们知道你吃了什么！

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:53.060
<v Kim>What? What did I eat?
<v Kim>什么？我吃了什么？

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:54.930
<v Detective>Chinese food!
<v Detective>中餐！

00:22:54.930 --> 00:23:01.230
<v Saul Elbein>And later on, Kim parroted that back to them. But most of what Jim saw was more subtle than that.
<v Saul Elbein>后来，金又把它鹦鹉学舌地还给了他们。但吉姆看到的大部分内容都比那更微妙。

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:14.360
<v Saul Elbein>Watching the interrogation, he saw that it had gone down like a long game of 20 Questions. She'd tell him something that didn't fit his theory and he'd say, no, that isn't right. What really happened? And she'd offer something else.
<v Saul Elbein>看着审讯过程，他看到它像一场漫长的“20个问题”游戏。她会告诉他一些不符合他理论的东西，他会说，不，那不对。到底发生了什么？她会提供别的东西。

00:23:14.360 --> 00:23:24.940
<v Saul Elbein>And if that worked, Jim would be really approving, and then that's the detail that he'd write down. And they'd move on until she'd given him a confession that totally fit his pre-existing theory of the crime.
<v Saul Elbein>如果那行得通，吉姆会非常赞同，然后那就是他会写下来的细节。他们会继续进行，直到她给了他一个完全符合他预先存在的犯罪理论的告解。

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:40.450
<v Saul Elbein>That's how Kim got the name of the drugstore called People's, where the credit card was also used. Jim asked her if she'd been there, and Kim said she didn't remember. He then said, you don't remember? And if you listen, just for an instant, you hear his tone turn disapproving.
<v Saul Elbein>金就是这样得到了叫“人民”的药店的名字，信用卡也在那里被使用了。吉姆问她是否去过那里，金说她不记得了。然后他说，你不记得了？如果你听一下，哪怕只是一瞬间，你都会听到他的语气变得不赞成。

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:42.610
<v Jim Trainum>Did you go a People's?
<v Jim Trainum>你去过人民药店吗？

00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:44.120
<v Kim>I don't remember.
<v Kim>我不记得了。

00:23:44.120 --> 00:23:45.580
<v Jim Trainum>You don't remember?
<v Jim Trainum>你不记得了？

00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:52.190
<v Saul Elbein>Kim picked up on that and changed her answer. She said, oh yeah. Yes, I did. I had to get something.
<v Saul Elbein>金听出了那个语气并改变了回答。她说，哦，是的。是的，我去了。我必须买点东西。

00:23:52.190 --> 00:23:54.790
<v Kim>Oh yeah. Yes, I did.
<v Kim>哦，是的。是的，我去了。

00:23:54.790 --> 00:23:55.950
<v Jim Trainum>What'd you buy from--
<v Jim Trainum>你买了什么——

00:23:55.950 --> 00:23:56.650
<v Kim>I had to get something.
<v Kim>我必须买点东西。

00:23:56.650 --> 00:24:10.290
<v Saul Elbein>There's only one piece they didn't give Kim. The bit about the shrimp dish. That seems to have been just an extremely unlucky guess.
<v Saul Elbein>只有一件东西他们没有给金。关于那道虾类菜肴的部分。那似乎只是一个极其不幸的猜测。

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:24.980
<v Saul Elbein>Jim and the other interrogators messed up in other ways, too. In addition to feeding her information, they ignored some really important information she was giving them. There are a few places on the tape where Kim said things that should have raised sudden, immediate doubts as to her guilt.
<v Saul Elbein>吉姆和其他审讯人员也在其他方面搞砸了。除了给她喂信息外，他们还忽略了她给他们的非常重要的信息。录像带有几个地方，金说的话本应引发对她有罪的突然、立即的怀疑。

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:39.670
<v Saul Elbein>Like this one scene in the video. An interrogator had crime scene pictures of the victim's hands. He let Kim take the photos from him and hold them up to her face. Her reaction wasn't quite what you'd expect from someone who was there. She immediately said, they tied him up!
<v Saul Elbein>比如录像里的这一幕。一名审讯人员手里有受害者手的犯罪现场照片。他让金从他那里拿走照片并举到脸前。她的反应并不完全像一个身临其境的人应该有的样子。她立即说，他们捆绑了他！

00:24:39.670 --> 00:24:41.580
<v Kim>They tied him. They tied him up!
<v Kim>他们捆绑了他。他们捆绑了他！

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:54.560
<v Jim Trainum>They tied him up! Now that's a pretty significant statement. I mean, because she's surprised about the bindings and all of that. But I mean, we never picked up on that, that she was surprised at that.
<v Jim Trainum>他们捆绑了他！那是一个非常重要的陈述。我是说，因为她对捆绑之类的东西感到惊讶。但我意思是，我们从来没有察觉到这一点，她对那个感到惊讶。

00:24:54.560 --> 00:25:08.730
<v Jim Trainum>And later on when she's talking to us about what happened, she talks about how he was tied up with this wire-like thing, about how they didn't take his wedding ring. All things that she could've picked up from the pictures.
<v Jim Trainum>后来当她和我们谈论发生的事情时，她谈到了他是如何被那种类似电线的东西捆绑的，谈到了他们没有拿走他的结婚戒指。所有这些都是她可以从照片中收集到的东西。

00:25:08.730 --> 00:25:23.730
<v Saul Elbein>But the most outrageous thing Jim missed when she told him, I was pregnant. I was seven months pregnant the night of the murder. This was important because the woman they were looking for, who had showed up in the ATM photo and had been seen by liquor store clerks, was clearly not pregnant.
<v Saul Elbein>但吉姆错过的一件最离谱的事情是，她说，我怀孕了。谋杀当晚我怀孕七个月了。这很重要，因为他们要寻找的那个出现在ATM照片中，并且被酒类商店职员看到的女人，明显没有怀孕。

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:30.980
<v Kim>I was pregnant. [CRYING] I was seven months pregnant then.
<v Kim>我怀孕了。[哭泣] 那时我怀孕七个月了。

00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:33.410
<v Saul Elbein>Wait. How did you not hear that?
<v Saul Elbein>等等。你是怎么没听到的？

00:25:33.410 --> 00:25:50.240
<v Jim Trainum>Where that went was right over top of my head. I mean, that was an alibi. And we just didn't pick up on it. Why? Because we weren't looking for it. We were only looking for confirmation of what we thought was true, rather than seeking out what the truth really is.
<v Jim Trainum>那东西直接从我头顶飞过去了。我是说，那是一个不在场证明。而我们就是没抓住它。为什么？因为我们没有去寻找它。我们只是在寻找证实我们认为正确的事情，而不是去寻求什么是真正的真相。

00:25:50.240 --> 00:26:02.020
<v Saul Elbein>There was no record of the pregnancy anywhere in Jim's notes. This was in spite of the fact that when he arrested her, she had been holding a three-week-old baby, meaning she was definitely very pregnant at the time of the murder.
<v Saul Elbein>吉姆的笔记里没有任何关于怀孕的记录。尽管事实上当他逮捕她时，她怀里抱着一个三周大的婴儿，这意味着她在谋杀案发生时确实非常显怀。

00:26:02.020 --> 00:26:15.460
<v Saul Elbein>But no one at the DC police did the math. They didn't feel like they had to. Kim had confessed.
<v Saul Elbein>但华盛顿特区的警察里没有人去做这个计算。他们不觉得他们必须这么做。金已经告解了。

00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:28.670
<v Saul Elbein>Here's what's really troubling. Jim learned all this stuff by accident. At the time, the overwhelming majority of interrogations in this country were not videotaped. Usually, the police only turned on a recorder when they were ready to tape the final confession.
<v Saul Elbein>真正令人不安的是这里。吉姆是偶然学到所有这些东西的。当时，这个国家绝大多数的审讯都没有被录像。通常，警察只在准备录制最终告解时才会打开录音机。

00:26:28.670 --> 00:26:44.650
<v Saul Elbein>But luckily, in Kim's interrogation, Jim and his partners messed up. After they took her confession for credit card fraud, Jim forgot to turn the recorder off. He just kept interviewing her while the video ran, which later gave him a very rare look at the way his interrogation had actually happened.
<v Saul Elbein>但幸运的是，在金的审讯中，吉姆和他的伙伴搞砸了。在他们记录了她的信用卡欺诈告解后，吉姆忘了关掉录音机。他只是在录像运行的时候继续审讯她，这后来让他非常罕见地看到了他的审讯实际上是如何发生的。

00:26:44.650 --> 00:26:56.800
<v Saul Elbein>And by the end of it, Jim saw why Kim's confession had sounded so completely convincing. They had inadvertently rehearsed for almost 17 hours. Kim appeared so cold blooded and unrepentant because she was just exhausted.
<v Saul Elbein>到最后，吉姆明白了为什么金的告解听起来如此完全令人信服。他们无意中排练了近17个小时。金看起来如此冷酷、毫无悔意，因为她只是精疲力竭。

00:26:56.800 --> 00:27:17.020
<v Saul Elbein>If the case hadn't fallen apart and had gone to trial, her final confession is all a jury would have seen, not the interrogation videos from much earlier where she was denying everything and getting help on the facts from the police. Jim is convinced she would have been convicted.
<v Saul Elbein>如果案件没有分崩离析并进入审判阶段，她的最终告解是陪审团唯一会看到的东西，而不是更早之前的审讯录像，那时她在否认一切并从警察那里得到事实帮助。吉姆确信她会被定罪。

00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:24.230
<v Saul Elbein>Once he figured all this out, Jim didn't go around telling everyone he knew that he'd gotten a false confession. At least, not at first.
<v Saul Elbein>一旦他弄明白了这一切，吉姆并没有四处告诉他认识的每个人他得到了一个虚假告解。至少，起初没有。

00:27:24.230 --> 00:27:45.740
<v Jim Trainum>I mean, I'll be honest with you. Who would I have told? My bosses were old school. I mean, they were the dinosaur's dinosaurs. And they would have a problem believing me if I told them that I thought it was a false confession. They would have thought, what's wrong with you? You're crazy.
<v Jim Trainum>我是说，我会对你诚实的。我能告诉谁呢？我的老板们是老派的。我是说，他们是恐龙中的恐龙。如果我告诉他们我认为这是一个虚假告解，他们会有麻烦相信我。他们会想，你怎么了？你疯了。

00:27:45.740 --> 00:27:51.950
<v Saul Elbein>Instead, he quietly pulled up her case file on the police's computer system and typed in that Kim's confession was false.
<v Saul Elbein>相反，他悄悄地在警察的电脑系统中调出了她的案卷，并输入了金的告解是虚假的。

00:27:51.950 --> 00:28:12.380
<v Jim Trainum>If anybody who came behind me-- let's say that I retired, I dropped dead or whatever and somebody came behind me-- I didn't want them bothering Kimberly at all. But later on, her name came up with another detective who came to me and said, look, I pulled up Kim's name in the computer and she was a murder suspect?
<v Jim Trainum>如果任何在我之后来的人——假设我退休了，我死了或者别的什么，有人接替我——我不希望他们打扰金伯利。但后来，她的名字出现在另一位侦探那里，他来找我说，看，我在电脑里调出了金的名字，她是一个谋杀嫌疑人？

00:28:12.380 --> 00:28:19.230
<v Jim Trainum>And I said, oh yeah. But didn't you read the last part? I ruled her out, whatever, and all that.
<v Jim Trainum>我说，哦是的。但你没读最后一部分吗？我排除了她，等等，所有这些。

00:28:19.230 --> 00:28:22.390
<v Jim Trainum>He said yeah, I read that. But she confessed.
<v Jim Trainum>他说，是的，我读了。但她告解了。

00:28:22.390 --> 00:28:31.270
<v Jim Trainum>So I said, listen to what I'm saying. It's a false confession. But it's like it couldn't penetrate.
<v Jim Trainum>所以我说，听我说的话。这是一个虚假告解。但就像它无法穿透一样。

00:28:31.270 --> 00:28:48.810
<v Saul Elbein>For a very long time, it's pretty much been gospel among law enforcement that a confession is a confession is a confession. When Jim went through interrogation training, he was taught that a person would never ever confess to something they didn't do, unless they'd been physically tortured or were mentally ill.
<v Saul Elbein>在很长一段时间里，执法部门一直把“告解就是告解就是告解”视为福音。当吉姆接受审讯培训时，他被教导一个人绝对绝对不会为一个他们没做过的事情告解，除非他们受到身体折磨或患有精神疾病。

00:28:48.810 --> 00:29:03.630
<v Saul Elbein>We've been talking about false confessions for 20 years now, and some people still don't believe in them. There have been cases where suspects falsely confessed, then recanted, backed up their innocence with DNA evidence, and were convicted anyway.
<v Saul Elbein>我们讨论虚假告解已经20年了，有些人仍然不相信它们。确实有一些嫌疑人虚假告解，然后推翻，用DNA证据支持他们的清白，却仍然被定罪的案例。

00:29:03.630 --> 00:29:31.350
<v Saul Elbein>Meanwhile, out in the police world, there's still some resistance to the one thing that most experts agree could help prevent wrongful convictions-- videotaping entire interrogations rather than just confessions. Only 17 states plus the District of Columbia have statutes or court rules on recording police interrogations in cases involving serious crimes.
<v Saul Elbein>与此同时，在警察世界里，对于大多数专家一致认为有助于防止错误定罪的一件事——录像整个审讯过程，而不是仅仅录制告解——仍然存在一些阻力。只有17个州加上哥伦比亚特区有关于在涉及严重犯罪的案件中记录警察审讯的法规或法院规则。

00:29:31.350 --> 00:29:45.390
<v Saul Elbein>When Jim retired from the DC Police in 2010, he'd basically become an evangelist for "I got a false confession and here's how." He started giving presentations about Kim's case to law schools and police conferences, where he pointed out all the ways he'd coached her.
<v Saul Elbein>当吉姆在2010年从特区警察局退休时，他基本上成为了“我得到了一个虚假告解，这是如何发生的”的传道者。他开始向法学院和警察会议做关于金案件的演讲，在会上他指出了他引导她的所有方式。

00:29:45.390 --> 00:30:02.240
<v Saul Elbein>But what's interesting is that when he gives those talks to police, he calls the class something like, "Avoiding Investigative Pitfalls" and then only at the very end slips in a bit about false confession. He said if he led with it, people would walk out.
<v Saul Elbein>但有趣的是，当他向警察做那些讲座时，他把课程命名为“避免调查陷阱”，然后只在最后悄悄加入了一点关于虚假告解的内容。他说如果他以那个为开头，人们会走掉。

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:13.020
<v Saul Elbein>Though Jim has been talking about Kim's case for years now, to all sorts of audiences, he has no idea whatever happened to her. I found her in another state, far away from Washington, DC.
<v Saul Elbein>虽然吉姆多年来一直谈论金的案子，面对各种听众，但他不知道她后来怎么样了。我在另一个州找到了她，离华盛顿特区很远。

00:30:13.020 --> 00:30:25.800
<v Saul Elbein>The only way she would meet me was in a hotel an hour away from where she lives. She was worried, she said. Kim lives in a small town, and people are nosy. She's spent a long time trying to put the past behind her.
<v Saul Elbein>她唯一愿意见我的方式是在离她住处一小时路程的一家酒店。她说她很担心。金住在一个小镇，人们很好奇。她花了很长时间试图把过去抛在身后。

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:38.440
<v Saul Elbein>She told me that when Jim first knocked on her door, she thought he had the wrong person. She tried telling him that, but Jim really had no interest in hearing her denials. So I asked her what she was thinking in that interrogation room 19 years ago.
<v Saul Elbein>她告诉我，当吉姆第一次敲她的门时，她以为他找错人了。她试着告诉他这一点，但吉姆真的对听她的否认没兴趣。所以我问她19年前在审讯室里在想什么。

00:30:38.440 --> 00:30:54.720
<v Kim>First thing that popped in my head was my kids. I just had a newborn. And that's when I started crying. I was starting to get more agitated. It's like, I didn't do this. I don't know what you guys are trying to do.
<v Kim>脑子里冒出的第一件事是我的孩子。我刚有一个新生儿。那时我开始哭。我开始变得更加激动。就像，我没做这个。我不知道你们试图做什么。

00:30:54.720 --> 00:31:04.730
<v Kim>I just want to get the process over with. I was tired. I just figured I'd give them something, and maybe they'd just let me go home.
<v Kim>我只是想结束这个过程。我很累。我只是想我会给他们一些东西，也许他们就会让我回家。

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:10.700
<v Kim>I don't know. I was 20 years old. That's what I thought was the best thing for me to do at that time.
<v Kim>我不知道。我20岁。那是那时我认为对我来说最好的做法。

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:17.670
<v Saul Elbein>The fact that she felt that way wasn't an accident. Jim said they implied they'd let her see her kids if she talked.
<v Saul Elbein>她有那种感觉并不是意外。吉姆说他们暗示如果她开口，就让她见她的孩子。

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:27.590
<v Saul Elbein>At one point, I asked Kim if it ever surprised her that she was talked into giving a false confession. She looked at me like I was crazy. She told me she hadn't confessed at all.
<v Saul Elbein>有一次，我问金她是否因为被说服做出虚假告解而感到惊讶。她看着我就像看着疯子一样。她告诉我她根本没有告解。

00:31:27.590 --> 00:31:36.500
<v Saul Elbein>A confession, she explained, means admitting to something you did. But she didn't do anything. Basically, Jim asked her to play a game and she played it.
<v Saul Elbein>她解释说，告解意味着承认你所做的事情。但她没做任何事。基本上，吉姆让她玩一个游戏，她就玩了。

00:31:36.500 --> 00:31:45.940
<v Kim>It was just something that I just made up by what I saw. I didn't really confess to anything.
<v Kim>那只是我根据我看到的编造出来的东西。我并没有真正告解任何事情。

00:31:45.940 --> 00:31:54.750
<v Saul Elbein>So I don't totally understand the difference. But it seems like it's an important one and I want to make sure I get it. You're saying you didn't make a confession. You told them something.
<v Saul Elbein>所以我不太明白其中的区别。但它似乎是一个重要的区别，我想确保我理解正确。你是说你没有做告解。你告诉了他们一些东西。

00:31:54.750 --> 00:32:06.010
<v Kim>I mean, if you put a bunch of pictures in front of a child and you tell them what comes first and what comes next? Nine times out of ten they're going to make up their own thing.
<v Kim>我是说，如果你把一堆图片放在孩子面前，你告诉他们什么先出现，什么后出现？十有八九他们会编造出自己的东西。

00:32:06.010 --> 00:32:15.710
<v Kim>I know I wasn't a child. But things were put in front of me, and I just told a story.
<v Kim>我知道我不是孩子。但东西放在我面前，我就讲了一个故事。

00:32:15.710 --> 00:32:27.630
<v Saul Elbein>Even Kim's court-appointed attorney had wanted her to plead guilty. Some of her family thought she'd done it. So even after she was released from jail, Kim found it impossible to get back on her feet.
<v Saul Elbein>甚至金的法庭指定律师也想让她认罪。她的一些家人认为她做了。所以即使在从监狱释放后，金也发现很难重新站起来。

00:32:27.630 --> 00:32:52.270
<v Saul Elbein>Child Protective Services took her kids. And without her kids, she lost her spot in the family shelter. Without any place to live, she says she was never stable enough to get her kids back. Kim says she spent almost a year and a half on the street, at one point sleeping in a tool shed in a DC Park. She never regained custody of her children.
<v Saul Elbein>儿童保护服务带走了她的孩子。没有孩子，她失去了在家庭收容所的位置。没有任何地方住，她说她从未稳定到能接回她的孩子。金说她在街上待了近一年半，有一次睡在华盛顿特区公园的一个工具棚里。她再也没有获得过孩子的监护权。

00:32:52.270 --> 00:33:07.760
<v Saul Elbein>Kim's case had been dismissed without prejudice, meaning if prosecutors ever found new evidence, they could bring her back in. Kim believes, although I couldn't verify this, that this prevented her from getting jobs, that the charge showed up on background checks, that people judged her for it.
<v Saul Elbein>金的案件在没有偏见的情况下被驳回，这意味着如果检察官发现新证据，他们可以把她带回去。金相信，虽然我无法核实这一点，但这阻止了她找到工作，指控出现在背景调查中，人们因此评判她。

00:33:07.760 --> 00:33:23.190
<v Kim>Even though I wasn't locked up and I was released, I still felt like I was still in jail, but on the outside world. I mean, I was still treated like I was a criminal. I lost my kids. I was homeless on the street, couldn't get a job.
<v Kim>即使我没有被关起来，我被释放了，但我仍然觉得我好像还在监狱里，但在外部世界。我是说，我仍然被当作罪犯对待。我失去了我的孩子。我无家可归，在街上，找不到工作。

00:33:23.190 --> 00:33:37.090
<v Kim>And I really tried my best to turn my life around. I really tried. And soon as they did that background check, it was a no-go. I even tried to join the military, and it came back.
<v Kim>而我真的尽我所能扭转我的生活。我真的试过。一旦他们做了背景调查，这就没戏了。我甚至尝试加入军队，结果也被退回来了。

00:33:37.090 --> 00:33:44.820
<v Kim>I'm haunted. I don't know if anything's going to come about. Is this going to be brought back up again? I don't know.
<v Kim>我被困扰着。我不知道会发生什么。这还会被提起来吗？我不知道。

00:33:44.820 --> 00:33:56.040
<v Saul Elbein>Keep in mind that Kim, by the standards of people who confess falsely, was extremely lucky. She was innocent, and she was released. In her case, in a way, the system worked.
<v Saul Elbein>请记住，金，以虚假告解者的标准来看，非常幸运。她是无辜的，她被释放了。在她的案件中，在某种程度上，系统起作用了。

00:33:56.040 --> 00:34:18.680
<v Saul Elbein>She doesn't feel lucky, though. She feels like her life has been ruined. While Jim has spent most of the last decade using Kim's story as a cautionary tale, Kim herself had no idea until I talked to her that he'd ever thought about it again, let alone that he thought she was innocent, or that he blamed himself. So when she found out about this, she stood up and walked slowly out of the room, sobbing.
<v Saul Elbein>不过她并不觉得自己幸运。她觉得她的生活被毁了。当吉姆在过去十年里大部分时间都在利用金的故事作为警示故事时，金本人在和我谈话之前根本不知道他还会再次想到它，更不用说他认为她是无辜的，或者他自责。所以当她发现这件事时，她站起来慢慢走出房间，啜泣着。

00:34:18.680 --> 00:34:35.330
<v Kim>He could have really, if he really wanted to, make things right. He knows how to. He's a former detective, or whatever he is now. I don't know. He knows how to get a hold of me, and that's the thing that bugs me the most. [SOBBING]
<v Kim>如果他真的想，他本可以真正地纠正错误。他知道怎么做。他是一位前侦探，或者他现在是什么。我不知道。他知道怎么联系我，这就是最让我烦恼的事情。[啜泣]

00:34:35.330 --> 00:34:52.050
<v Kim>I can't change my past. I don't have any of my kids because of it. It's still on my record.
<v Kim>我无法改变我的过去。我因为这个失去了所有的孩子。它仍然在我的记录上。

00:34:52.050 --> 00:34:55.020
<v Kim>[PHONE RINGING]
<v Kim>[电话铃声]

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:57.010
<v Kim>Hello?
<v Kim>你好？

00:34:57.010 --> 00:34:59.470
<v Jim Trainum>Kim? This is Jim Trainum.
<v Jim Trainum>金？我是吉姆·特雷纳姆。

00:34:59.470 --> 00:35:07.860
<v Saul Elbein>When I interviewed Kim, she surprised me by asking for Jim's contact information. A week later, they ended up connecting by phone. They let me record.
<v Saul Elbein>当我采访金时，她问我要吉姆的联系方式，这让我很惊讶。一周后，他们最终通过电话联系上了。他们让我录音。

00:35:07.860 --> 00:35:17.970
<v Jim Trainum>I just want to first open by saying that I am very sorry for what you had gone through back then and all the pain that it caused you and problems that it's caused you.
<v Jim Trainum>我首先想开场说，我非常抱歉你当时经历的一切，以及它给你带来的所有痛苦和问题。

00:35:17.970 --> 00:35:32.810
<v Saul Elbein>In the years since he figured out his mistake, Jim had often thought about contacting Kim. But at the end, he decided not to. He consulted with psychologists who cautioned him about the impacts on Kim if he reached out to her. Plus, he told me, he was also a little scared.
<v Saul Elbein>在他发现错误的这些年里，吉姆经常想到联系金。但最后，他决定不这样做。他咨询了心理学家，他们提醒他如果他接触金会对她产生什么影响。另外，他告诉我，他也有点害怕。

00:35:32.810 --> 00:35:55.010
<v Saul Elbein>As you might expect, it was sort of an awkward conversation. Jim sounded really nervous. And for the first hour, he sounded like he was giving one of his academic speeches on how investigations go wrong. He used a lot of terms like "verification bias" and "contamination" to try and explain how false confessions happen, how he and his officers had messed up. He went on and on like this.
<v Saul Elbein>如你所料，那是一个有点尴尬的对话。吉姆听起来真的很紧张。在第一个小时里，他听起来就像在做关于调查如何出错的学术演讲。他用了许多诸如“验证偏见”和“污染”之类的术语，试图解释虚假告解是如何发生的，他和他的警官们是如何搞砸的。他就这样一直说下去。

00:35:55.010 --> 00:36:11.640
<v Saul Elbein>Through it all, it sounded like he wanted her to understand that they had meant well. They had tried their best, it just hadn't been good enough. To me, it sounded like he was saying, it's my mistake, but it wasn't completely my fault. Kim listened patiently, but after a while she cut in.
<v Saul Elbein>自始至终，听起来他想让她明白他们是出于好意。他们尽了最大的努力，只是不够好。对我来说，听起来他是在说，这是我的错误，但不是完全我的错。金耐心地听着，但过了一会儿她插话了。

00:36:11.640 --> 00:36:26.710
<v Kim>I'm not trying to seem like I'm being rude or anything. But you've got to understand after 20 years, 19 years, whatever years it is, you explaining the case to me, for what?
<v Kim>我不想让自己看起来很粗鲁或什么。但你必须明白，20年后，19年后，不管多少年过去了，你向我解释这个案子，为了什么？

00:36:26.710 --> 00:36:29.190
<v Jim Trainum>I'm not expecting you to forgive me, OK?
<v Jim Trainum>我没指望你原谅我，好吗？

00:36:29.190 --> 00:36:35.360
<v Kim>You were doing your job. If you knew all this, why didn't you try to find me?
<v Kim>你在做你的工作。如果你知道所有这些，为什么你不试着找到我？

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:45.480
<v Jim Trainum>I mean, I'll be honest with you. It may have been the wrong decision. All I can do is do what I can from this point on.
<v Jim Trainum>我是说，我会对你诚实的。这可能是一个错误的决定。我现在能做的就是从这一点开始做我能做的。

00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:52.990
<v Saul Elbein>Kim didn't care about his hand wringing. She'd been there. She knew how it happened. She just wanted Jim to help her get her record expunged.
<v Saul Elbein>金不在乎他的苦恼。她身临其境。她知道它是如何发生的。她只是想让吉姆帮她把记录删掉。

00:36:52.990 --> 00:36:55.930
<v Kim>Well, I do want this record thing taken care of.
<v Kim>好吧，我确实想把这个记录的事处理掉。

00:36:55.930 --> 00:36:56.230
<v Jim Trainum>OK.
<v Jim Trainum>好的。

00:36:56.230 --> 00:36:58.100
<v Kim>And I can't do it without any help.
<v Kim>没有帮助我做不到。

00:36:58.100 --> 00:37:13.170
<v Jim Trainum>I have a contact. I have a person in the public defender's office who does that sort of thing. I will work with him. And I will provide him with an affidavit, explain in the affidavit how you are innocent.
<v Jim Trainum>我有一个联系人。我在公设辩护人办公室有一个人做那种事。我会和他合作。我会给他提供一份宣誓书，在宣誓书中解释你是如何无辜的。

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:22.070
<v Kim>I can tell in a way that you're sincere. But it's going to take me time to start trusting.
<v Kim>我能看出某种程度上你是真诚的。但这需要时间让我开始信任。

00:37:22.070 --> 00:37:38.860
<v Saul Elbein>Jim also offered to help Kim find counseling, to help put her in touch with other people who had given false confessions, or who study them. And that's when the conversation started to change. 19 years ago, he'd tried to convince her she was guilty. Now he was trying to convince her it wasn't her fault.
<v Saul Elbein>吉姆还提议帮助金找到咨询，帮助她与做出虚假告解或研究它们的其他人取得联系。那就是对话开始改变的时候。19年前，他试图说服她她有罪。现在他试图说服她这不是她的错。

00:37:38.860 --> 00:37:48.560
<v Kim>I mean, so many people ask me, but why did you even say that you did it? So it kind of comes back on me as like I'm the stupid one.
<v Kim>我是说，很多人问我，但你为什么甚至说你做了它？所以这有点回到我身上，好像我是那个笨蛋。

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:55.900
<v Jim Trainum>No. No, you're not the stupid one. You were manipulated. I mean, you were doing what, at that time, you thought was best.
<v Jim Trainum>不。不，你不是那个笨蛋。你被操纵了。我是说，你当时做的是你认为最好的。

00:37:55.900 --> 00:38:15.240
<v Jim Trainum>None of this, none of this, is on you. Not one thing. I mean, people may try to say, well, I would never have said that! Well, you  know what? Yes, you would've. In the right circumstance, you would have. I promise you that.
<v Jim Trainum>这一切，这一切，都不是你的错。一件都没有。我是说，人们可能会试着说，好吧，我绝不会那样说！好吧，你知道吗？是的，你会的。在那种情况下，你也会。我向你保证。

00:38:15.240 --> 00:38:25.090
<v Kim>Yeah, I guess it just takes a little bit for it to all soak in for me not to feel the way I feel.
<v Kim>是的，我想这只是需要一点时间让它全部渗透，让我不再有现在的感觉。

00:38:25.090 --> 00:38:49.770
<v Saul Elbein>I listened to them talk without breaking in. Years ago, in an interrogation room, each of them did something they never would've thought they'd do. And now here they were again, their situations reversed. Jim was confessing to her.
<v Saul Elbein>我听着他们谈话而没有打断。多年前，在审讯室里，他们每个人都做了他们认为自己绝不会做的事情。而现在他们又在这里，他们的处境互换了。吉姆在向她告解。

00:38:49.770 --> 00:38:52.210
<v Ira Glass>Saul Elbein lives in Austin, Texas.
<v Ira Glass>索尔·埃尔本住在得克萨斯州奥斯汀。

00:38:52.210 --> 00:39:16.940
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "VIDEOTAPE" BY RADIOHEAD]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 电台司令的《录像带》]

00:39:16.940 --> 00:39:31.600
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, talking to the police may mess up your life, even if you're innocent. But what happens when you try not to talking to them? That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，和警察谈话可能会搞砸你的生活，即使你是无辜的。但当你尝试不和他们谈话时会发生什么？那将在几分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:38.670
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, "Confessions."
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们选择某个主题，带给你关于那个主题的各种故事。今天的节目，“告解”。

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:51.740
<v Ira Glass>So in Act One of today's show, you heard about a person who's wrongly accused of murder who gives a false confession. This next story is about a person who is also wrongly accused of murder, only in this case, they do not confess.
<v Ira Glass>所以在今天节目的第一幕，你听到了一个被诬告谋杀的人做出了虚假告解。下一个故事是关于一个同样被诬告谋杀的人，只是在这种情况下，他们没有告解。

00:39:51.740 --> 00:39:55.990
<v Ira Glass>This is Act Two. Act Two, "You Don't Say."
<v Ira Glass>这是第二幕。第二幕，“你别说”。

00:39:55.990 --> 00:40:13.750
<v Ira Glass>The murder in this act is a particularly horrible one. In Nashville in 1975, a nine-year-old girl named Marcia Trimble went out to deliver some Girl Scout cookies, and she did not come home. She was found dead a month later-- strangled, sexually assaulted.
<v Ira Glass>这一幕中的谋杀案是一起特别可怕的案件。1975年在纳什维尔，一个叫玛西娅·特林布尔的九岁女孩出去送女童军饼干，她没有回家。一个月后她被发现死亡——被勒死，遭受性侵。

00:40:13.750 --> 00:40:23.760
<v Ira Glass>Nashville at the time had not seen many crimes like this. It shook the whole city. Volunteer groups and Boy Scout troops searched for her. It was one of those crimes that made people rethink how safe their city was.
<v Ira Glass>那时的纳什维尔还没见过多少这样的罪行。它震惊了整个城市。志愿者团体和童子军部队搜索了她。这是那种让人重新思考他们的城市有多安全的罪行之一。

00:40:23.760 --> 00:40:44.700
<v Ira Glass>The case went unsolved for decades. And then in 2009, thanks to a DNA match, a convicted rapist named Jerome Barrett was found guilty in Marcia's death. But all that time, more than 30 years, law enforcement officials suspected that a different guy had committed the crime. That person was Jeffrey Womack.
<v Ira Glass>案件几十年来未破。然后在2009年，由于DNA比对，一个叫杰罗姆·巴雷特的被定罪的强奸犯在玛西娅的死亡案件中被判有罪。但所有那些时间，超过30年，执法官员怀疑是另一个家伙犯下的罪行。那个人就是杰弗里·沃马克。

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:53.280
<v Ira Glass>Jeffrey Womack was 15 when he became a suspect in Marcia Trimble's murder. He was 49 when Jerome Barrett was convicted.
<v Ira Glass>杰弗里·沃马克成为玛西娅·特林布尔谋杀案的嫌疑人时才15岁。当杰罗姆·巴雷特被定罪时，他已经49岁了。

00:40:53.280 --> 00:41:11.780
<v Ira Glass>Womack didn't speak publicly about the ordeal for years, until last year, when he and his attorney co-wrote a memoir and appeared in a documentary about the case. Before that, as producer Lisa Pollak explains, Jeffrey Womack was known, for better or for worse, not for what he did say, but for what he wouldn't say.
<v Ira Glass>沃马克多年来没有公开谈论过这次磨难，直到去年，当他和他的律师合写了一本回忆录并出现在一部关于该案的纪录片中时。在此之前，正如制片人丽莎·波拉克解释的那样，杰弗里·沃马克之所以闻名，无论好坏，不是因为他说了什么，而是因为他拒绝说什么。

00:41:11.780 --> 00:41:33.680
<v Lisa Pollak>I don't think I fully understood what it meant to be Jeffrey Womack, to be a suspect in a murder you didn't commit, until I talked to a woman named Demetria Kalodimos. She's a TV news anchor and investigative reporter in Nashville. Demetria told me that in 1984, when she first moved to Nashville, she went to a restaurant with her coworkers, and one of her colleagues spotted Jeffrey Womack in the kitchen.
<v Lisa Pollak>在我和一个叫德米特里亚·卡洛迪莫斯的女人交谈之前，我不认为我完全理解成为杰弗里·沃马克是什么意思，成为你没有犯下的谋杀案的嫌疑人。她是纳什维尔的一位电视新闻主播和调查记者。德米特里亚告诉我，在1984年，当她第一次搬到纳什维尔时，她和同事去了一家餐厅，她的一个同事在厨房里发现了杰弗里·沃马克。

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:42.150
<v Demetria Kalodimos>Someone whispered over to me and said, see that guy over there cooking? He killed a nine-year-old girl.
<v Demetria Kalodimos>有人悄悄对我说，看到那边做饭的那个家伙了吗？他杀了一个九岁的小女孩。

00:41:42.150 --> 00:41:53.600
<v Demetria Kalodimos>And I looked and I thought, wow, what's he doing cooking? Why is he here? And they said, well, it's a long story. They just couldn't get him, but they will.
<v Demetria Kalodimos>我看了看，我想，哇，他在做什么饭？他为什么在这里？他们说，好吧，这说来话长。他们只是没能抓住他，但他们会抓住的。

00:41:53.600 --> 00:42:13.020
<v Lisa Pollak>At this point, in 1984, it had been four years since the murder charges against him had been dropped for lack of evidence. And two years since the district attorney who'd issued those charges told the newspaper that he no longer believed Jeffrey had anything to do with the crime. And yet, people still believed that Jeffrey Womack had gotten away with murder.
<v Lisa Pollak>此时，在1984年，距离撤销对他的谋杀指控已经四年了。距离发布这些指控的地区检察官告诉报纸他不再相信杰弗里与该罪行有任何关系已经两年了。然而，人们仍然相信杰弗里·沃马克逍遥法外了。

00:42:13.020 --> 00:42:28.700
<v Lisa Pollak>Jeffrey wasn't the only suspect in Marcia Trimble's murder, but the cops had had their eyes on him from the night she went missing. They believed that the person who killed her was a young white male, probably a teenager from Marcia's neighborhood, a quiet, middle-class area called Green Hills.
<v Lisa Pollak>杰弗里不是玛西娅·特林布尔谋杀案中唯一的嫌疑人，但警察从她失踪的那晚起就一直盯着他。他们相信杀死她的人是一个年轻白人男性，可能是来自玛西娅社区的一个十几岁男孩，一个叫绿山的安静、中产阶级区域。

00:42:28.700 --> 00:42:45.960
<v Lisa Pollak>Jeffrey was all of those things. He was 15. He and Marcia lived on the same street. And on the day she disappeared, Jeffrey was one of the last people to see her. She'd come to his house collecting money for Girl Scout cookies. He told her he didn't have money to pay her, but that he'd bring it by in a while.
<v Lisa Pollak>杰弗里具备所有这些特质。他15岁。他和玛西娅住在同一条街上。在她失踪的那天，杰弗里是最后见到她的人之一。她来到他家收女童军饼干的钱。他告诉她他没钱付给她，但他过一会儿会送过去。

00:42:45.960 --> 00:43:10.190
<v Lisa Pollak>Later that night, when he heard Marcia was missing, he went to her house to tell the cops that he'd seen her. He figured it would be a quick conversation. But the way he remembers it, three cops took a look at him, his long hair, his smart alecky attitude, and the shoes in which he'd written [BLEEP] you in ballpoint pen, and started interrogating him right there in the house about where he'd been that day, asking him to account for his time in five-minute increments.
<v Lisa Pollak>那天晚上晚些时候，当他听说玛西娅失踪时，他去她家告诉警察他见过她。他以为那会是一次简短的谈话。但据他记得，三个警察看了他一眼，他那头长发，他那种自以为是的态度，以及他用圆珠笔写着“[哔]你”的鞋子，就开始在房子里审讯他，问他那天去过哪里，要求他以五分钟为增量说明他的时间。

00:43:10.190 --> 00:43:11.790
<v Jeffrey Womack>They were very aggressive.
<v Jeffrey Womack>他们非常激进。

00:43:11.790 --> 00:43:12.870
<v Lisa Pollak>This is Jeffrey.
<v Lisa Pollak>这是杰弗里。

00:43:12.870 --> 00:43:26.230
<v Jeffrey Womack>I just know that when it was over, I was so confused about why all these questions like this? They'd ask them one way and they'd ask them another way. And I couldn't ever get the answer right.
<v Jeffrey Womack>我只知道当它结束时，我很困惑为什么会有所有这些问题？他们以一种方式问，他们又以另一种方式问。而且我从来没能得到正确的答案。

00:43:26.230 --> 00:43:34.590
<v Lisa Pollak>I should tell you that police deny they grilled Jeffrey. One of the three detectives, Tommy Jacobs, told me they only questioned him for 15 minutes, and that it wasn't contentious.
<v Lisa Pollak>我应该告诉你警察否认他们审问了杰弗里。其中一名探员，汤米·雅各布斯，告诉我他们只盘问了他15分钟，而且并不激烈。

00:43:34.590 --> 00:43:39.060
<v Jeffrey Womack>Then they asked me to take off my coat, told me to empty my pockets.
<v Jeffrey Womack>然后他们让我脱掉外套，告诉我清空我的口袋。

00:43:39.060 --> 00:43:42.540
<v Demetria Kalodimos>And at the point when they had him empty his pockets--
<v Demetria Kalodimos>而在他们让他清空口袋的那一点——

00:43:42.540 --> 00:43:47.180
<v Lisa Pollak>Again, Demetria Kalodimos. Last year she made a documentary about the Trimble case.
<v Lisa Pollak>再次是德米特里亚·卡洛迪莫斯。去年她制作了一部关于特林布尔案的纪录片。

00:43:47.180 --> 00:43:52.500
<v Demetria Kalodimos>That visual never left the heart of this case.
<v Demetria Kalodimos>那个视觉画面从未离开过这个案件的核心。

00:43:52.500 --> 00:43:55.760
<v Jeffrey Womack>I had half rolls of pennies in my pocket and a five-dollar bill.
<v Jeffrey Womack>我口袋里有半卷便士和一张五美元钞票。

00:43:55.760 --> 00:44:01.430
<v Lisa Pollak>Remember, he told the cops that he didn't have money to pay for Girl Scout cookies. And now here he was with money.
<v Lisa Pollak>记住，他告诉警察他没钱付女童军饼干。而现在他在这里有了钱。

00:44:01.430 --> 00:44:03.180
<v Jeffrey Womack>And I had a condom.
<v Jeffrey Womack>而且我有一个避孕套。

00:44:03.180 --> 00:44:18.140
<v Demetria Kalodimos>The clincher. He's got a condom. And what's a 15-year-old doing with a condom? And a little girl's missing, and that just sounds too unsavory.
<v Demetria Kalodimos>定论。他有一个避孕套。一个15岁的人拿着避孕套在做什么？而且一个小女孩失踪了，那听起来太令人反感了。

00:44:18.140 --> 00:44:37.010
<v Lisa Pollak>The real reason why Jeffrey had a condom might also sound unsavory. Unbeknownst to his parents, he was sleeping with one of the moms in the neighborhood. What happened next, at least how Jeffrey tells it, is that his mom got word that her son was being interrogated in a bedroom at the Trimbles' house, and she and their next-door neighbor, Peggy, headed over there to put an end to it.
<v Lisa Pollak>杰弗里手里拿着避孕套的真正原因可能听起来也很反感。在父母不知情的情况下，他正在与附近的一位妈妈发生性关系。接下来发生的事情，至少是杰弗里讲述的方式，是他的妈妈听说她的儿子正在特林布尔家的卧室里接受审讯，她和他们的隔壁邻居佩吉一起赶过去结束它。

00:44:37.010 --> 00:44:44.340
<v Jeffrey Womack>Peggy just pushed the door open and just made a statement, if you want to talk to him, you can talk to his lawyer.
<v Jeffrey Womack>佩吉只是推开门并声明，如果你想和他谈，你可以和他的律师谈。

00:44:44.340 --> 00:45:03.790
<v Demetria Kalodimos>And that's really when things changed. Just the mention of having a lawyer automatically put even more suspicion over Jeffrey. The thinking was, no one's accused him of anything. Why does he need an attorney?
<v Demetria Kalodimos>而那才是事情真正改变的时候。仅仅提到拥有律师就自动让杰弗里受到了更多的怀疑。当时的逻辑是，没有人指控他任何事情。他为什么需要律师？

00:45:03.790 --> 00:45:11.330
<v Lisa Pollak>The next day, Jeffrey found himself face to face with John Hollins, a well-known attorney in Nashville. He'd represented Peggy in her divorce.
<v Lisa Pollak>第二天，杰弗里发现自己面对的是约翰·霍林斯，纳什维尔一位著名的律师。他曾代表佩吉处理离婚案。

00:45:11.330 --> 00:45:22.110
<v Jeffrey Womack>He asked me to tell him, tell me exactly what happened yesterday. And I told him. And I told him what happened at the Trimble house.
<v Jeffrey Womack>他让我告诉他，告诉我昨天到底发生了什么。我告诉了他。我告诉了他特林布尔家发生的事情。

00:45:22.110 --> 00:45:27.300
<v Lisa Pollak>And then he gave Jeffrey an instruction that would come to define both the case and his life.
<v Lisa Pollak>然后他给了杰弗里一个将定义该案件和他生活的指示。

00:45:27.300 --> 00:45:38.890
<v Jeffrey Womack>He said, do not talk to these people anymore. Do not talk to any more detectives. Tell them talk to me.
<v Jeffrey Womack>他说，不要再和这些人说话了。不要再和任何侦探说话。告诉他们和我说话。

00:45:38.890 --> 00:45:55.540
<v Lisa Pollak>So Jeffrey didn't talk. And since he wasn't talking, police tried other ways to get information out of him. When he was 17, working in a restaurant, an undercover cop joined the staff as a dishwasher, hung out with Jeffrey, went drinking with him to see if he'd say anything about the Trimble case.
<v Lisa Pollak>所以杰弗里没有说话。由于他不说话，警察尝试其他方法从他那里获取信息。当他17岁时，在一家餐厅工作，一名卧底警察作为洗碗工加入员工行列，和杰弗里混在一起，和他一起去喝酒，看看他是否会说出关于特林布尔案的任何事情。

00:45:55.540 --> 00:46:07.370
<v Lisa Pollak>Another coworker wore a wire and asked Jeffrey point blank if he killed Marcia. He said no. Once, on the anniversary of her death, they staked out the cemetery in case Jeffery went to Marcia's grave. He didn't.
<v Lisa Pollak>另一位同事戴着窃听器，直截了当地问杰弗里是否杀了玛西娅。他说没有。有一次，在她去世的周年纪念日，他们监视了墓地，以防杰弗里去玛西娅的墓地。他没去。

00:46:07.370 --> 00:46:17.280
<v Lisa Pollak>Finally, four a half years after the murder, investigators had enough evidence-- evidence that later fell apart-- to arrest Jeffrey. They brought him in in the middle of the night.
<v Lisa Pollak>最终，在谋杀案发生四年半后，调查人员有了足够的证据——后来分崩离析的证据——逮捕杰弗里。他们在半夜把他带走。

00:46:17.280 --> 00:46:32.720
<v Lisa Pollak>When I talked to the arresting officer, she was adamant that no one pressured Jeffrey to talk or even asked him for a statement. But Jeffrey says they did, that one officer taunted him about how he supposedly strangled Marcia, how he grabbed her by the throat and wouldn't let go. And then he was given an ultimatum.
<v Lisa Pollak>当我与逮捕警官交谈时，她坚持认为没有人施压让杰弗里说话，甚至没有要求他做声明。但杰弗里说他们做了，一名警官嘲弄他，说他如何设想勒死了玛西娅，如何抓住她的喉咙并不放手。然后他被下了最后通牒。

00:46:32.720 --> 00:46:41.860
<v Jeffrey Womack>This is your last chance to talk to us. If you want to talk to us, you better talk to us now. I said, OK, I'll talk to you.
<v Jeffrey Womack>这是你和我们谈话的最后机会。如果你想和我们谈话，你最好现在和我们谈话。我说，好吧，我和你们谈。

00:46:41.860 --> 00:46:55.450
<v Jeffrey Womack>And she grabbed her pen and pad. As a matter of fact, there was a couple other officers in there. I can't remember. And I just looked at them and said, [BLEEP] you.
<v Jeffrey Womack>她抓起她的笔和记事本。事实上，还有几个警察也在里面。我记不清了。我只是看着他们说，[哔]你。

00:46:55.450 --> 00:47:01.070
<v Lisa Pollak>For years, Jeffrey says, the question followed him. If he was innocent, why wouldn't he just talk to police?
<v Lisa Pollak>多年来，杰弗里说，问题一直困扰着他。如果他是无辜的，他为什么不和警察谈谈？

00:47:01.070 --> 00:47:12.960
<v Jeffrey Womack>A lot of people were friends of my parents that wanted me to go down and talk to the district attorney. Wanted me to go talk to the police department, straighten all this out, make it end. This is killing your parents.
<v Jeffrey Womack>很多人是我父母的朋友，想让我下去和地区检察官谈谈。想让我去警察局谈谈，理清这一切，让它结束。这正在害死你的父母。

00:47:12.960 --> 00:47:19.880
<v Jeffrey Womack>But that wasn't the thing to do. My best friend, Doug Green-- all you need to do is sit down and talk to these people. No!
<v Jeffrey Womack>但那不是该做的事。我最好的朋友，道格·格林——你所要做的就是坐下来和这些人谈谈。不！

00:47:19.880 --> 00:47:21.960
<v Lisa Pollak>Well, your best friend told you to talk to the cops?
<v Lisa Pollak>好吧，你最好的朋友让你和警察谈谈？

00:47:21.960 --> 00:47:33.580
<v Jeffrey Womack>Yeah. He wanted me to sit down and talk to them. He thought I could sit down and talk to them and straighten it all out. They wanted me to sit down and talk to them and confess to it.
<v Jeffrey Womack>是的。他想让我坐下来和他们谈谈。他认为我可以坐下来和他们谈谈并理清这一切。他们想让我坐下来和他们谈谈并认罪。

00:47:33.580 --> 00:47:46.780
<v Jeffrey Womack>There was two different things. They didn't want to hear from me that I had nothing to do with it. That's not what they wanted to hear. So I really had nothing to say to them. It was useless for me to speak to them.
<v Jeffrey Womack>那是两码事。他们不想从我这里听到我与此事无关。那不是他们想听的。所以我真的没什么话对他们说。对我来说，和他们说话是徒劳的。

00:47:46.780 --> 00:47:55.620
<v Lisa Pollak>It didn't help Jeffrey's reputation that he kept his mouth shut while the cops talked to reporters. But even when he was given permission to tell his side, Jeffrey didn't take it.
<v Lisa Pollak>当警察和记者谈话时，他保持沉默，这对杰弗里的声誉没有帮助。但即使在他被允许说出他的立场时，杰弗里也没有这么做。

00:47:55.620 --> 00:48:01.720
<v Alan Griggs>Today, for the first time, Jeffrey Womack talked publicly about the Trimble murder investigation and his part in it.
<v Alan Griggs>今天，杰弗里·沃马克首次公开谈论特林布尔谋杀案调查以及他在其中的角色。

00:48:01.720 --> 00:48:08.220
<v Lisa Pollak>This is an interview from right after the murder charges against Jeffrey were dropped. The perfect time, you might think, to proclaim himself an innocent victim.
<v Lisa Pollak>这是一次在针对杰弗里的谋杀指控被撤销后不久的采访。你可能认为这是他宣布自己是无辜受害者的最佳时机。

00:48:08.220 --> 00:48:14.550
<v Alan Griggs>By his choice, Womack did not elaborate on most of the questions, deferring to the lawyers in some cases.
<v Alan Griggs>按他的选择，沃马克没有详述大多数问题，在某些情况下推脱给律师。

00:48:14.550 --> 00:48:26.370
<v Male Reporter>Anything you would like to say that you've been wanting to get off your back for these many years now that rumors have been flying and stories have going about your situation?
<v Male Reporter>现在谣言满天飞，关于你的处境的故事不断，有什么你想说的话，你想把它从你的背上卸下来吗？

00:48:26.370 --> 00:48:29.730
<v Jeffrey Womack>No.
<v Jeffrey Womack>不。

00:48:29.730 --> 00:48:32.860
<v Male Reporter>Nothing at all?
<v Male Reporter>一点都没有？

00:48:32.860 --> 00:48:36.110
<v Alan Griggs>Alan Griggs, Channel Four News.
<v Alan Griggs>艾伦·格里格斯，第四频道新闻。

00:48:36.110 --> 00:48:39.830
<v Demetria Kalodimos>We'd heard a lot about him, but we never heard a lot from him.
<v Demetria Kalodimos>我们听到了很多关于他的传闻，但我们从未听过他自己说很多话。

00:48:39.830 --> 00:48:42.200
<v Lisa Pollak>Again, Demetria Kalodimos.
<v Lisa Pollak>再次是德米特里亚·卡洛迪莫斯。

00:48:42.200 --> 00:48:47.190
<v Lisa Pollak>Do you think the silence made him look guiltier to people?
<v Lisa Pollak>你认为沉默让他在人们看来更像是有罪的吗？

00:48:47.190 --> 00:48:59.730
<v Demetria Kalodimos>People will tell you that. Well, this guy's obviously guilty. I mean, look at him. He doesn't say a word. He looks straight ahead. He doesn't seem to show any emotion.
<v Demetria Kalodimos>人们会告诉你那点。好吧，这家伙显然有罪。我是说，看看他。他一句话也不说。他直视前方。他似乎没有表现出任何情绪。

00:48:59.730 --> 00:49:18.310
<v Demetria Kalodimos>And I think that was a feeling of a lot of reporters covering the case, and it was the feeling certainly of the audience we were presenting it to. I've always thought if it were me, I'd be yelling from the rooftops. I wouldn't be listening to my lawyer and staying behind closed doors and never saying another word.
<v Demetria Kalodimos>我认为那是许多报道此案的记者的感觉，而且这肯定是我们将它呈现给的观众的感觉。我一直认为如果是我，我会从屋顶上大喊大叫。我不会听我的律师的话，呆在闭门造车，再也不说一句话。

00:49:18.310 --> 00:49:34.370
<v Lisa Pollak>When I asked Jeffrey's attorney, John Hollins, if he worried that the silence hurt Jeffrey, made him seem unsympathetic to people, he said, I wasn't worried about him being sympathetic. I was worried about them trying to put him in the penitentiary.
<v Lisa Pollak>当我问杰弗里的律师约翰·霍林斯，他是否担心沉默伤害了杰弗里，让他看起来不值得同情时，他说，我不担心他是否值得同情。我担心的是他们试图把他送进监狱。

00:49:34.370 --> 00:49:47.280
<v Lisa Pollak>It probably won't surprise you that both the detectives I talked to said they weren't out to get Jeffrey, that they were following leads, trying to eliminate suspects or build a case, if there was one to build. They said if Jeffrey had talked to them, maybe they could have cleared him.
<v Lisa Pollak>我不担心那会让你吃惊，我和他们交谈的两名侦探都说他们并不是要针对杰弗里，他们是在跟踪线索，试图排除嫌疑人或建立案件，如果有案件可建的话。他们说如果杰弗里和他们谈过，也许他们就能洗清他的嫌疑。

00:49:47.280 --> 00:50:04.770
<v Lisa Pollak>But Jeffrey and his lawyer both believe that not talking is what kept him free. For Jeffrey, not talking had other consequences. There were people who gave him the cold shoulder or talked behind his back once they learned he was that Jeffrey Womack. He told me a story about a woman he once liked, somebody who used to come drink at the restaurant where he worked.
<v Lisa Pollak>但杰弗里和他的律师都认为不说话是他保持自由的原因。对杰弗里来说，不说话还有其他后果。有些人因为他知道他是那个杰弗里·沃马克后，对他冷眼相待或在背后议论他。他告诉我一个他曾经喜欢的女人的故事，一个曾经来餐厅喝酒的女人。

00:50:04.770 --> 00:50:17.120
<v Jeffrey Womack>And we just started hanging out with each other. And she freaked out when she found out who I was. I just can't hang around with you anymore, because of who you are.
<v Jeffrey Womack>然后我们刚开始和对方闲逛。当她发现我是谁时，她吓坏了。我不能再和你闲逛了，因为你是谁。

00:50:17.120 --> 00:50:19.290
<v Lisa Pollak>Well, how did you feel when she said that to you?
<v Lisa Pollak>好吧，当她对你这么说时，你感觉如何？

00:50:19.290 --> 00:50:32.290
<v Jeffrey Womack>I felt bad. Because I couldn't explain how to get out of it, how to make it where, don't worry about it. It's OK. How can I defend myself for something I didn't do?
<v Jeffrey Womack>我感觉很糟糕。因为我无法解释如何摆脱它，如何让它变得，别担心它。没关系。我怎么能为我没有做过的事情辩护？

00:50:32.290 --> 00:50:50.280
<v Lisa Pollak>You said there is no defense. But there is a defense. You didn't want to say, I didn't do it. I'm the one who's being wronged here. Why not defend yourself at all to people and make your case? You had a case to make.
<v Lisa Pollak>你说没有辩护。但有一个辩护。你不想说，我没做。我是那个被冤枉的人。为什么不向人们为自己辩护并提出你的案件？你有一个案件要提。

00:50:50.280 --> 00:51:10.680
<v Jeffrey Womack>What you're saying sounds good. But when you sit down and you start talking to someone about this, it's, well, how did you get in this mess? Even when I would say I had nothing to do with it, well, you must have done something because they wouldn't keep coming after if you didn't do anything wrong. The police department doesn't do that.
<v Jeffrey Womack>你说的听起来不错。但当你坐下来开始和某人谈论这件事时，就是，好吧，你卷入这个混乱是怎么回事？即使当我说我与它无关时，好吧，你一定做了什么，因为如果他们没有做错任何事，他们就不会继续追你。警察局不会那样做。

00:51:10.680 --> 00:51:27.790
<v Jeffrey Womack>Well, they did do that. The horrible fact of the whole thing was that they did. So you learn to shut up about it. You learn to stop.
<v Jeffrey Womack>好吧，他们确实那样做了。整件事可怕的事实是他们确实这样做了。所以你学会了闭嘴。你学会了停止。

00:51:27.790 --> 00:51:43.060
<v Lisa Pollak>Jeffrey is 54 now. He doesn't think the Trimble case ruined his life. And when people suggest it has, he doesn't like it. He doesn't want to play the victim, and he doesn't want people psychoanalyzing him. Listen to how he reacted when Demetria, during one of our interviews, brought up the V word.
<v Lisa Pollak>杰弗里现在54岁了。他不认为特林布尔案毁了他的生活。当人们建议它毁了时，他不喜欢。他不想扮演受害者，他不想人们分析他。听听当德米特里亚在我们采访中提到V词（受害者）时，他是如何反应的。

00:51:43.060 --> 00:51:46.090
<v Demetria Kalodimos>Do you think of yourself as a victim sometimes?
<v Demetria Kalodimos>你有时候会认为自己是受害者吗？

00:51:46.090 --> 00:51:51.100
<v Jeffrey Womack>I don't know. I really don't know.
<v Jeffrey Womack>我不知道。我真的不知道。

00:51:51.100 --> 00:51:54.280
<v Kathy>I do. Just think about what your life would have been if you even--
<v Kathy>我是。想想如果你哪怕——你的生活会是什么样

00:51:54.280 --> 00:51:59.320
<v Lisa Pollak>That's Kathy, an old friend of Jeffrey's in the background. She saying, just think of what your life would have been.
<v Lisa Pollak>那是凯西，杰弗里在后台的一位老朋友。她在说，想想你的生活会是什么样。

00:51:59.320 --> 00:52:08.550
<v Jeffrey Womack>Don't know. Don't know what my life would have been. I just don't know. Because what do you think your life would have been? Who knows?
<v Jeffrey Womack>不知道。不知道我的生活会是什么样。我只是不知道。因为你认为你的生活会是什么样？谁知道呢？

00:52:08.550 --> 00:52:17.450
<v Lisa Pollak>Jeffrey's had his share of hard stuff in his life. He went through a divorce. He was a heavy drinker. He's been to rehab. But he won't blame the murder case for any of that.
<v Lisa Pollak>杰弗里在他生活中经历了不少困难。他经历过离婚。他曾是个酗酒者。他去过康复中心。但他不会把那些归咎于谋杀案。

00:52:17.450 --> 00:52:32.810
<v Lisa Pollak>In the past 30 years, Jeffrey says, there were times he'd go months without even thinking about the Trimble case. And then, inevitably, there'd be a news story about it, the mystery that had never been solved. And the same old questions would come up again. And sometimes even his own mother wasn't sure what to think.
<v Lisa Pollak>在过去的30年里，杰弗里说，有时他会几个月都不去想特林布尔案。然后，不可避免地，会有一个关于它的新闻报道，从未解决的谜团。同样的老问题又会出现。有时甚至连他自己的母亲都不确定该怎么想。

00:52:32.810 --> 00:52:52.930
<v Jeffrey Womack>She'd be very upset, and she'd be crying. And it's hard to explain to your mother that you had nothing to do with this after they repeatedly, for 20-something years, kept saying Jeffrey Womack's got something to hide. Jeffrey Womack knows something. Jeffrey Womack's still a subject.
<v Jeffrey Womack>她会非常心烦，她会哭。而当你告诉你的母亲你与此无关后，他们一再地，在20多年里，一直说杰弗里·沃马克有什么隐瞒。杰弗里·沃马克知道什么。杰弗里·沃马克仍然是一个主题。

00:52:52.930 --> 00:53:04.150
<v Jeffrey Womack>And then she would say, do you know anything about this? No. You sure? You don't know anything about this?
<v Jeffrey Womack>然后她会说，你对此知道什么吗？不。你确定？你对此什么都不知道？

00:53:04.150 --> 00:53:22.750
<v Jeffrey Womack>It was hard. That was the hardest part. I could handle what anybody else would say. It was heartbreaking that telling her once I didn't have anything to do with it wasn't enough.
<v Jeffrey Womack>那很难。那是最难的部分。我可以处理任何人会说的任何话。心碎的是，告诉她一次我与此无关是不够的。

00:53:22.750 --> 00:53:39.140
<v Lisa Pollak>It wasn't until 2009, when another man was tried and convicted for killing Marcia, that Jeffrey was fully and undeniably cleared. The case was solved when detectives discovered that the DNA on Marcia Trimble's shirt matched the DNA of a convicted rapist named Jerome Barrett.
<v Lisa Pollak>直到2009年，当另一个人因为杀害玛西娅受审并被定罪时，杰弗里才被完全且无可辩驳地洗清罪名。案件解决时，侦探发现玛西娅·特林布尔衬衫上的DNA与一个叫杰罗姆·巴雷特的被定罪强奸犯的DNA匹配。

00:53:39.140 --> 00:54:06.140
<v Lisa Pollak>Even though Barrett had been arrested for other sexual assaults near Trimble's neighborhood near the time of her death, and was charged with murdering a student the same month she disappeared, the Nashville police had never considered Barrett a suspect. Remember, they'd been looking for a white teenage boy from the neighborhood. And Jerome Barrett was black, an adult, and lived nowhere near the Trimbles. But even a DNA match was not enough to convince everybody in Nashville that Jeffrey's innocent. Again, Demetria Kalodimos.
<v Lisa Pollak>即使巴雷特在玛西娅死亡时间附近在特林布尔社区附近因其他性侵犯被捕，并被指控谋杀了一名学生在同一月份她失踪，纳什维尔警察从未考虑巴雷特为嫌疑人。记住，他们一直在寻找一个来自社区的十几岁白人男孩。杰罗姆·巴雷特是黑人，成年人，住在离特林布尔家很远的地方。但即使是DNA比对也不足以让纳什维尔的所有人都相信杰弗里是无辜的。再次是德米特里亚·卡洛迪莫斯。

00:54:06.140 --> 00:54:23.230
<v Demetria Kalodimos>At the time when we were putting the documentary together, I thought that everyone would be convinced. And I was frankly amazed at some of the comments that I still got from people saying, yeah, but he worked with Jerome Barrett, and they probably knew each other. And I mean, there are still people who will not let this go.
<v Demetria Kalodimos>当时我们组装纪录片时，我以为每个人都会相信。而我坦率地对人们仍然给我的评论感到惊讶，他们说，是的，但他和杰罗姆·巴雷特一起工作，他们可能认识彼此。我的意思是，仍然有不会放过这件事的人。

00:54:23.230 --> 00:54:27.270
<v Lisa Pollak>Demetria says the cops she's interviewed haven't expressed much sympathy for Jeffrey.
<v Lisa Pollak>德米特里亚说，她采访过的警察没有对杰弗里表示太多同情。

00:54:27.270 --> 00:54:44.600
<v Demetria Kalodimos>Every single one of them said he asked for what he got. Everything he got was his fault. If he had just talked to us, if he had just did this, if he had just done that. There's never been any sort of apology.
<v Demetria Kalodimos>他们中的每一个都说他自找的。他得到的一切都是他的错。如果他早点和我们谈，如果他早点做了这个，如果他早点做了那个。从来没有过任何形式的道歉。

00:54:44.600 --> 00:55:02.630
<v Lisa Pollak>Jeffrey's not holding out hope for an apology, and he probably shouldn't. In July, the weekly Nashville Scene published its annual "You Are So Nashville If" issue, and one of the items on the list was, you are so Nashville if you still haven't apologized to Jeffrey Womack.
<v Lisa Pollak>杰弗里不指望道歉，他可能也不应该指望。七月，周刊《纳什维尔场景》出版了其年度“如果你是如此纳什维尔”版，清单上的项目之一是，如果你仍然没有向杰弗里·沃马克道歉，那么你是如此纳什维尔。

00:55:02.630 --> 00:55:20.000
<v Ira Glass>Lisa Pollak is one of the producers of our show. Jeffrey Womack and his attorney have told their story in a book called The Suspect , A Memoir . Their ghost writer, who's a Nashville journalist named E. Thomas Wood, got unprecedented access to the Marcia Trimble police file during his reporting, and we drew on his research for our story. Our thanks to him.
<v Ira Glass>丽莎·波拉克是我们节目的制片人之一。杰弗里·沃马克和他的律师在书《嫌疑人，回忆录》中讲述了他们的故事。他们的代笔者，一位叫E.托马斯·伍德的纳什维尔记者，在报道期间获得了对玛西娅·特林布尔警局档案的前所未有的访问权限，我们从他的研究中获得了我们故事的素材。感谢他。

00:55:20.000 --> 00:55:27.200
<v Ira Glass>Demetria Kalodimos's documentary on the Trimble case is called Indelible , The Case Against Jeffrey Womack. There's a link to it on our website.
<v Ira Glass>德米特里亚·卡洛迪莫斯关于特林布尔案的纪录片叫《不可磨灭，针对杰弗里·沃马克的案件》。我们的网站上有它的链接。

00:55:27.200 --> 00:55:54.850
